Q21. Semantic Deduplication & Cosine Similarity Filtering

SCENARIO: Scraped datasets often contain duplicated or near-identical text blocks. Ingesting redundant chunks wastes vector database storage and skews search results.

TASK: Write a deduplication function in Python that takes a list of dense vector embeddings and drops chunks when cosine similarity exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.92).

TOPICS: Medium+, Math, Deduplication

SOLUTION:
import numpy as np
from typing import List, Dict, Any, Tuple

def deduplicate_embeddings(
    chunks: List[Dict[str, Any]], 
    embeddings: np.ndarray, 
    threshold: float = 0.92
) -> Tuple[List[Dict[str, Any]], np.ndarray]:
    norms = np.linalg.norm(embeddings, axis=1, keepdims=True)
    normalized = embeddings / np.maximum(norms, 1e-12)

    keep_indices = []
    for i in range(len(chunks)):
        is_duplicate = False
        for j in keep_indices:
            similarity = np.dot(normalized[i], normalized[j])
            if similarity >= threshold:
                is_duplicate = True
                break
        if not is_duplicate:
            keep_indices.append(i)

    deduped_chunks = [chunks[i] for i in keep_indices]
    deduped_embeddings = embeddings[keep_indices]
    return deduped_chunks, deduped_embeddings
